#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "961a5c28-9e1e-5b0c-bd04-63068bebd8b4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/id #uuid "2f02b5d4-640f-5212-af28-66c572d47b37" :question/math-academy-id "e-10828" :question/problem "Express $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t288^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $288^\\circ,$ which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/4c/4c60c3aecabe8f625d09843ae5bdbc1e7e5525c1f0e6ab894387320c5420cab8.png)\n\nTo express $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)$ in terms of $\\sin\\theta_R,$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = \\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{8\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pi }{5}-\\frac{8\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7d5897-3360-59fb-9f64-2f9f641290be" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "0a1428c6-96e8-5c82-82b2-ed51375454ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10829" :question/problem "Express $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t252^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $252^\\circ,$ which lies in the $3\\textrm{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/56/564054c68a924449a5730ee69f028ffe5e1b202ad8cb6ad4738b254873046ee5.png)\n\nTo express $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)$ in terms of $\\cos\\theta_R,$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{5}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\cos\\theta_R = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right) = -\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e677d9a-0be2-548e-ac29-b35be42f42ac" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "91e32306-d234-5db7-acd3-3b6d5bd8f437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10830" :question/problem "Express $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\theta_R.$" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi} {3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t240^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{4\\pi} {3}$ is equivalent to $240^\\circ,$ which lies in the $3\\textrm{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/2a/2a6cc3cdec7887e13b502c49e3a0d5af98dda860789a5ffaef6105fdb5e477c4.png)\n\nTo express $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\tan\\theta_R,$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right),$ and therefore we're interested in $\\tan\\theta_R = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$ must be *positive* because the tangent ratio is *always* positive in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1519905-a736-54a1-8778-cc03d019a084" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "5eb9b588-a263-5a0f-a3a2-96efe4bd8d08" :question/math-academy-id "e-10831" :question/problem "Express $\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\\right) = 117^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}$ is equivalent to $117^\\circ,$ which lies in the $2\\textrm{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/5f/5f059e8447f65aa1b321eb24665e36dd7a3a4a1628ccfdd73412acefa9ce4ee3.png)\n\nTo express $\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)$ in terms of $\\cot\\theta_R,$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = \\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{13\\pi }{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\cot\\theta_R = \\cot\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{20}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\cot\\left( \\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the 2nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\cot\\left( \\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right) = -\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{20}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e677d9a-0be2-548e-ac29-b35be42f42ac" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76bdef9c-f236-54e8-8836-4212e9f21172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566589} {:answer/id #uuid "894f79ee-fbb9-59fd-a530-5bbcfc38d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566624} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9909aa-f561-5e4b-8e6f-a2dd3fe32fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566703} {:answer/id #uuid "b1037d50-5d56-585f-b784-5cc78be970d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566715} {:answer/id #uuid "f78d3c3a-e9ac-5f4f-9f6d-cd0c62e1f5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1037d50-5d56-585f-b784-5cc78be970d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566715} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd002646-d5bf-5b7f-a764-6a47744e5a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7c752ad-04c5-54cf-9d34-168623b90a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-156044" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={112.5}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${112.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/9b/9b4b14f758b27c3bf9af0edec9fd6125fa268e4ec90b9138a986bb81afd48fc5.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e677d9a-0be2-548e-ac29-b35be42f42ac" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4500506d-0dc2-5226-b244-060346fe3405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566499} {:answer/id #uuid "8b93ef7a-8561-50f5-a232-6af32efb32cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566628} {:answer/id #uuid "93421e03-12b6-5005-87f8-a6ee58a03475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566651} {:answer/id #uuid "95354370-942a-523e-9f8d-b8a4123c078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566657} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c91f1c-6eb6-5a8d-b48b-51444ed941ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95354370-942a-523e-9f8d-b8a4123c078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f932fed-397b-5ace-b980-5e7fbe127e60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f664604-518d-5102-b04f-ac66274e4b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-156064" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={225}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to ${225}^{∘},$ which lies in the $3\\text{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d676fb0c9d75ee8230340203ec1a853e34384061ce899ddfc0185e49d2f8dbec.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ must be *positive* because the tangent ratio is *always* positive in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1519905-a736-54a1-8778-cc03d019a084" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0362914a-2ba9-5072-860b-691c4653e0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566367} {:answer/id #uuid "24e234fa-fe7c-5c3d-aa0b-3bb70d1b07b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566441} {:answer/id #uuid "af519233-270c-5ed0-a399-5951152787df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566712} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbed2a2-cfc0-5c31-b2b0-68ccffcdd435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566766} {:answer/id #uuid "e4102b19-e608-50b0-be66-9cc06acf5fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af519233-270c-5ed0-a399-5951152787df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566712} :answer-field/id #uuid "717667f3-97e6-59a4-923d-5196f9eb79aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9d502b-5792-5f23-8fe5-325b77e48b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-156095" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={144}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{4\\pi }{5}$ is equivalent to ${144}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/d9/d9a7c21097737d5b9bcfcf81f7127651bb43bae15c77b617ef6e2ef039a339e5.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{4\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})$ must be positive because sine (and therefore cosecant) is always positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1519905-a736-54a1-8778-cc03d019a084" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e13dadc-ff81-528e-9c5a-0d82b2fa5105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566427} {:answer/id #uuid "2910a118-12d0-55a3-948e-ca1de0a828a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566452} {:answer/id #uuid "367a6d87-71d2-5ccd-b226-361c1c030e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566471} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6c1e2a-9d6a-584e-9fca-c8ec90fb2654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566600} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f2a19e-4ab6-5e5e-8696-8cf066687b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "367a6d87-71d2-5ccd-b226-361c1c030e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566471} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cd172d-4fe9-5312-8123-d8cc7c56d90d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d922afd-bca6-504f-94e4-0636e349c3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-156110" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={132}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is equivalent to ${132}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/23/23a979aa6a346c2572ecc108e3b88ac4cbe3f192dc6818c975c9373cbca33e74.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{15})=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "961a5c28-9e1e-5b0c-bd04-63068bebd8b4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2792cd75-5a84-53d7-8491-5b91dacadb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566448} {:answer/id #uuid "42d06c19-f46b-5173-8df1-4ae98d82170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566495} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7ef754-bbbc-5d5a-95b7-520a5ed902a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566771} {:answer/id #uuid "d748f2bb-f458-5ea8-90c9-04e882560096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566793} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e5a366-5d3c-5341-bf17-782bcbad1d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce7ef754-bbbc-5d5a-95b7-520a5ed902a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566771} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1c585e-3588-50e2-ad40-f55869456e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc822143-a882-56e7-a63c-7b3ea241404a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156141" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={135}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to ${135}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/bb/bb29311d0e31708c7a3d9c8eb356b12531cb2e99af10a02bdf653c167346b438.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$  must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "961a5c28-9e1e-5b0c-bd04-63068bebd8b4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008bf75e-050b-58ab-8f2d-0f365eb7a7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566360} {:answer/id #uuid "039cf306-0126-5764-bf19-e0c056dcde07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566368} {:answer/id #uuid "78e6b7ad-d19e-52f9-b190-3f25cb33dd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566593} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b6f41e-5756-5a52-8836-75961b8058f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566791} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e00566-bce8-541f-87f8-6188a4ebb0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e00566-bce8-541f-87f8-6188a4ebb0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566863} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f4d91a-c3cf-512e-99f9-72c16dd087ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c2e099a-ce97-51bf-a567-98e410e87149" :question/math-academy-id "q-156176" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={337.5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${337.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/a2/a248eaf8b200e2788db08164f77c2d3d457837e851726f54ca22f19cbc1dc440.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{15\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{8})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7d5897-3360-59fb-9f64-2f9f641290be" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edfe9a2-e8ae-503f-bbdc-4826f3e0aeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566604} {:answer/id #uuid "9f02033c-763b-5e0c-8fd5-990a639d71c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566671} {:answer/id #uuid "b6da8054-6941-55a6-9867-496063ddc2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566725} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f1c93-ff3f-59e8-90e0-3827c745eefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566731} {:answer/id #uuid "bb643e90-15c2-54f0-86b3-996299e8ff3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edfe9a2-e8ae-503f-bbdc-4826f3e0aeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566604} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f89144-238e-52a6-a3f7-a494782e66b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7db8f68b-e6bd-517c-b7bb-afe40f692949" :question/math-academy-id "q-156216" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${150}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/d0/d04b5b75e4406104bd2621d54be389e87cb9e289199aac60db449fe3244a9b45.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is in the $2$nd  quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7d5897-3360-59fb-9f64-2f9f641290be" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040b22cc-cfeb-583b-81a5-53f0dea47107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566369} {:answer/id #uuid "78edad60-37a0-5696-8808-78c28387a1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566594} {:answer/id #uuid "8d07ca4b-f8eb-5021-8b28-28339236a06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566633} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc566ec-859a-5f21-abf9-9853edc71d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566741} {:answer/id #uuid "d77afca8-c208-5bf5-94d2-109895490fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d77afca8-c208-5bf5-94d2-109895490fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566794} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f4340c-600d-5355-bdc0-562f920def5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9712142-63a5-5fa8-9ab4-6ab895057764" :question/math-academy-id "q-156218" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is equivalent to ${120}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd  quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is in the $2$nd  quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7d5897-3360-59fb-9f64-2f9f641290be" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5137f900-8819-5d2b-870d-9336f074e8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566516} {:answer/id #uuid "8049c500-a93f-583f-9bcd-8b17d834f7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566607} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f420e8-7277-5e0a-aae2-bb9d6c1656a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566683} {:answer/id #uuid "d28d88ff-ec44-58d2-9a11-04081769a148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566777} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c2a08c-128b-51b8-a4de-683e8f8d49cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f420e8-7277-5e0a-aae2-bb9d6c1656a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566683} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1bb264d-a67d-5a32-839a-5ed29fa27f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db4f44d3-9afa-5f71-9446-7889414ad8ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-156232" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{10})$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{11\\pi }{10})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={198}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${198}^{∘},$ which lies in the $3$rd quadrant.\n\n![](images/4f/4f66f8119626f97d6439aee81f5a02b1893a4dc2a787addc6c4a71ff865e3687.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{10})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{10})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{10})$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{10})=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}).\n$$"}]}
